ref 17592186492357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492775 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b06a318c-820e-5ff0-97ca-8c71655f9348" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492777 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492532 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492777 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492547 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492777 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492570 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492777 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492603 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492777 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492663 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492777 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492777 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492777 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186492532 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492777 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "b313a6a7-5aef-5eef-9fdc-9a3f6384635e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492790 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186492273 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492790 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492790 #edb/ref 1077 "field-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492790 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186492273 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492790 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d11afbdb-9351-5d55-af3d-0ebc6b7799e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492872 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186492734 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492872 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186492762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492872 #edb/ref 1069 "q-251783" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492872 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492872 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/7a/7ac752bbc51ea08ba8723fa0aba111d3f302cc149db8762208ae18ed31be02d1.png)\n\n**From left to right**, the values of the $x$-intercepts of the parabola shown above are{{field-1}}\nand \n{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492872 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "735e4f99-1ffc-57e4-b137-966d99c9aba8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492872 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(1,-4)$ and passes through the point $(0,-3).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(1,-4)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =4p(y+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(0,-3),$ we substitute $x=0$ and $y=-3$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-1)^{2} & =4p(-3+4) \\\\ 1 & =4p(1) \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =y+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =0+4 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =4 \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{4} \\\\ x & =\\pm 2+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the $x$-intercepts are $-1$  and $3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492924 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186492777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492924 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79303" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492924 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492924 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/84/84fa3bc1d70144b0fd2d1865e784ed1cee9d8b83633547f2e62be1a30e4242aa.png)\n\nWhat is the $y$-intercept of the parabola shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492924 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f789e17e-9222-50f7-90e6-3a489f3b51f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492924 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(2,-5)$ and passes through the point $(-2,3).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(2,-5)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}=4p(y+5).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-2,3),$ we substitute $x=-2$ and $y=3$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2-2)^{2} & =4p(3+5) \\\\ 16 & =4p(8) \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =2(y+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-2)^{2} & =2(y+5) \\\\ 4 & =2(y+5) \\\\ y & =2-5 \\\\ y & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $-3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492926 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186492726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492926 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186492775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492926 #edb/ref 1069 "q-240667" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492926 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492926 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/ac/ac9ee9825678ad3ba815204c39ac2fe4ea85972cc60458251fcc9e9fdb4ba0f7.png)\n\n**From left to right**, the **exact values** of the $x$-intercepts of the parabola shown above are{{field-1}}and{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492926 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f822983e-1d14-50b6-989d-68e7f1c76860" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492926 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-1,3)$ and passes through the point $(-2,\\frac{5}{2}).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(-1,3)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =4p(y-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-2,\\frac{5}{2}),$ we substitute $x=-2$ and $y=\\frac{5}{2}$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2+1)^{2} & =4p(\\frac{5}{2}-3) \\\\ (-1)^{2} & =4p(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ 1 & =-2p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =-2(y-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =-2(0-3) \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =6 \\\\ x+1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{6} \\\\ x & =-1\\pm \\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the $x$-intercepts are $-1-\\sqrt{6}$ and $-1+\\sqrt{6}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492928 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186492747 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492928 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186492790 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492928 #edb/ref 1069 "q-251773" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492928 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492928 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/75/7515041ccdceb8d13e4e718bda12af7535cfbb4a37b0180b04f88a08db4947b2.png)\n\n**From left to right**, the values of the $x$-intercepts of the parabola shown above are{{field-1}}\nand \n{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492928 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fdc8ed74-44a5-549d-abf7-39e6c995f550" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492928 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(3,-1)$ and passes through the point $(0,\\frac{5}{4}).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(3,-1)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4p(y+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(0,\\frac{5}{4}),$ we substitute $x=0$ and $y=\\frac{5}{4}$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-3)^{2} & =4p(\\frac{5}{4}+1) \\\\ 9 & =4p(\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\ p & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4(y+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4(0+1) \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =4 \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm \\sqrt{4} \\\\ x & =\\pm 2+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the $x$-intercepts are $1$  and $5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492971 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186492993 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492971 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "96d654a5-c93d-56e0-932c-bfd42b4b3f25" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492971 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186492928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492979 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a55bc8a3-ad49-5dfc-b4d0-974e21a65508" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492979 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186441439 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492993 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186492979 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492993 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ca9516a2-5640-5521-9384-7fc046858e73" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186492993 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186492872 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493003 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186493005 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493003 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ec4c1f2e-ee02-534f-8b4d-4d02bf4356e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493003 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186492924 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493005 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186492971 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493005 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f42eea9a-325b-5c56-95b4-dc3b76529b04" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493005 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186492926 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
